        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "fix(cron): publish removals after durable commit",
        "body": "Fixes #104078.\n\n## What Problem This Solves\n\nFixes an issue where external cron mirrors could observe a one-shot job as removed before OpenClaw had durably committed that deletion. If the final store write failed, the observer could cancel a wake for a job that still existed on disk, while the live Gateway could temporarily expose the speculative deletion.\n\n## Why This Change Was Made\n\n- Buffer automatic `removed` events for manual runs, normal timer runs, and startup catch-up until the final durable write succeeds.\n- Restore the complete live cron topology, durable next-wake snapshot, and catch-up deferral ownership when that write fails.\n- Keep the completed deleted-job snapshot for the eventual event, and remove an unreachable invalid-persisted-job deletion branch.\n- Document `scheduled` and `removed` hook events as post-commit reconciliation hints.\n\nThe change preserves existing `started` and `finished` timing. It does not turn lifecycle hooks into an ordered delta stream; consumers should still reconcile the full durable cron view.\n\n## User Impact\n\nPlugin authors and host integrations can now treat `cron_changed` removal notifications as durable deletion facts. A failed cron-store write emits no removal, and subsequent cron reads continue to expose the still-durable job.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- Blacksmith Testbox `tbx_01kx7fqgk4tjw6e9zw6dar21gs`: six new manual, timer, and startup-catch-up success/failure regressions passed.\n- Same Testbox: 195 focused cron and Gateway hook tests passed across the new suite, one-shot behavior, startup catch-up, cron ops, timer regressions, and Gateway cron integration.\n- Same Testbox: path-scoped `check:changed` passed, including core and test typechecks, lint, import-cycle, database-first storage, and runtime-sidecar guards.\n- Source-blind live Gateway proof passed twice with distinct one-shot jobs: each emitted exactly `started` → `finished` → `removed`, the removed snapshot exactly matched the completed job snapshot, and loopback Admin `cron.list` already omitted the job inside the removal callback and afterward.\n- Source-blind final-write fault injection was blocked: documented filesystem and SQLite locking surfaces could not safely isolate the deletion commit. The six focused tests provide the deterministic failed-write/no-removal/rollback proof.\n- Docs MDX, i18n glossary, and formatting checks passed; targeted oxfmt and `git diff --check` passed.\n- Fresh autoreview completed with no accepted/actionable findings (`patch is correct`, 0.93).\n\nRelease-note context: automatic cron removals now publish only after durable deletion succeeds and retain the full deleted-job snapshot.\n\nAI-assisted implementation; maintainer-reviewed against current `main`. No agent transcript is included.\n",
